WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.185
Lesson 1.2.

00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:04.490
How a transaction actually happens.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.624
By the end, you'll be able to follow

00:00:06.624 --> 00:00:09.797
a transaction from signature to finality, and estimate

00:00:09.797 --> 00:00:12.220
what it actually costs before you send it.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.810
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:18.007
First, the full lifecycle, every stop a transaction

00:00:18.007 --> 00:00:21.505
makes between your signature and being irreversible.

00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:23.248
Second, gas: what it is, and

00:00:23.248 --> 00:00:25.170
the two parts of its price.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.508
Third, the nonce, and why one stuck

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:30.405
transaction can jam every one behind it.

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.773
And finally, the real math: what a

00:00:32.773 --> 00:00:35.750
swap actually costs, quiet versus congested.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.255
The full lifecycle.

00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:41.310
Here's the complete path.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.618
You create the transaction, specifying what it does.

00:00:44.618 --> 00:00:46.633
You sign it with your private key.

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:49.048
Your wallet broadcasts it to the network.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.560
It sits in the mempool, a

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.128
public waiting room, visible to anyone.

00:00:53.128 --> 00:00:55.443
A validator includes it in a block.

00:00:55.443 --> 00:00:57.498
The network confirms that block.

00:00:57.498 --> 00:01:01.071
And after enough confirmations, it reaches finality: the

00:01:01.071 --> 00:01:04.393
point after which it can't realistically be reversed.

00:01:05.693 --> 00:01:08.831
Here's the short version, the one worth keeping in

00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:12.393
your head day to day: sign, mempool, block, finality.

00:01:12.393 --> 00:01:14.880
Gas is paid whether the transaction succeeds

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.091
or fails, because the network still did

00:01:17.091 --> 00:01:18.718
the work of checking it.

00:01:18.718 --> 00:01:21.270
That single fact explains most of what's

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.008
counterintuitive about how DeFi charges you.

00:01:25.308 --> 00:01:28.394
One more distinction worth holding onto: “pending”

00:01:28.394 --> 00:01:31.053
and “confirmed” are not the same claim.

00:01:31.053 --> 00:01:33.165
Pending means the network has seen your

00:01:33.165 --> 00:01:35.788
transaction and it's waiting in the mempool.

00:01:35.788 --> 00:01:38.428
Confirmed means it made it into a block.

00:01:38.428 --> 00:01:41.956
Until it's confirmed, anyone watching the mempool, including

00:01:41.956 --> 00:01:44.732
a rival bidder, can technically see what you're

00:01:44.732 --> 00:01:46.941
about to do and try to act first.

00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:49.276
That's part of why priority tips exist.

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:53.501
Notice the word “realistically” in finality.

00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:56.885
On most chains, a transaction can technically still be

00:01:56.885 --> 00:02:00.631
reorganised for a short window right after confirmation.

00:02:00.631 --> 00:02:03.642
Waiting for a few more confirmations, or for the

00:02:03.642 --> 00:02:06.775
finality rules of the specific chain and layer two

00:02:06.775 --> 00:02:09.970
you're using, which you'll cover in Module Five, is

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.366
what makes “final” actually mean final.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:14.291
Gas.

00:02:15.591 --> 00:02:18.166
Gas is the unit of computation.

00:02:18.166 --> 00:02:21.250
Every operation a transaction performs, reading a

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.396
balance, updating a balance, checking a signature,

00:02:24.396 --> 00:02:26.423
costs a fixed amount of gas.

00:02:26.423 --> 00:02:28.059
Your total cost is gas

00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:30.494
used, multiplied by gas price.

00:02:30.494 --> 00:02:33.299
Both numbers matter, and they move independently.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.645
Gas price itself has two parts, on Ethereum.

00:02:37.645 --> 00:02:39.699
The base fee, set by the network

00:02:39.699 --> 00:02:41.504
based on how busy it is, and

00:02:41.504 --> 00:02:44.170
burned, destroyed, not paid to anyone.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.418
And the priority tip, an extra amount

00:02:46.418 --> 00:02:48.666
you add to get included sooner, which

00:02:48.666 --> 00:02:50.445
does go to the validator.

00:02:50.445 --> 00:02:52.955
Gas price is quoted in gwei: one

00:02:52.955 --> 00:02:55.389
gwei is one billionth of an ETH.

00:02:56.689 --> 00:03:00.339
One real pattern worth knowing: priority tips spike

00:03:00.339 --> 00:03:03.849
hardest during sudden demand, a popular NFT mint,

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:07.078
a hyped token launch, everyone racing for the

00:03:07.078 --> 00:03:09.694
same limited block space at once.

00:03:09.694 --> 00:03:13.022
That's a real, recurring phenomenon on Ethereum and

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:15.690
similar chains, not a rare edge case.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.895
If you're not in a race for a scarce,

00:03:17.895 --> 00:03:21.143
time-limited slot, there's usually no reason to pay the

00:03:21.143 --> 00:03:23.870
premium everyone else is paying in that moment.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.758
Gas price isn't fixed, it's an auction for

00:03:27.758 --> 00:03:30.585
block space, and it follows real patterns.

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.728
Weekday business hours in the US and Europe tend

00:03:33.728 --> 00:03:36.551
to run busier, and so does any period right

00:03:36.551 --> 00:03:39.930
after major news or a popular new app launching.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.844
Quiet periods, like weekends and the middle of the

00:03:42.844 --> 00:03:45.830
night in those time zones, tend to run cheaper.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.328
None of this is guaranteed on any given

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.827
day, but if a fee looks unusually high,

00:03:50.827 --> 00:03:53.075
waiting an hour is often free money.

00:03:54.375 --> 00:03:55.465
Quick check.

00:03:55.465 --> 00:03:57.870
Your transaction fails and reverts.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.165
Did you still pay for it?

00:04:03.585 --> 00:04:05.622
The answer: yes, for the gas used

00:04:05.622 --> 00:04:07.540
up to the point of failure.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.566
The network did the computation, even

00:04:09.566 --> 00:04:11.485
though the result didn't go through.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:13.610
The nonce.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.293
Every account's transactions are numbered,

00:04:17.293 --> 00:04:19.200
in order, starting from zero.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.955
That number is the nonce.

00:04:20.955 --> 00:04:24.712
The network processes them strictly in sequence: it won't

00:04:24.712 --> 00:04:28.146
process nonce six until nonce five has confirmed, no

00:04:28.146 --> 00:04:31.126
matter how much you're willing to pay for six.

00:04:32.426 --> 00:04:35.326
So here's what a stuck transaction actually does.

00:04:35.326 --> 00:04:37.796
You send transaction five with too low a

00:04:37.796 --> 00:04:40.806
fee, and it sits in the mempool, unconfirmed.

00:04:40.806 --> 00:04:44.036
You send transactions six and seven right after.

00:04:44.036 --> 00:04:46.646
They also sit and wait, even though they're

00:04:46.646 --> 00:04:49.971
perfectly valid, because five hasn't confirmed yet.

00:04:49.971 --> 00:04:52.607
The fix is to replace transaction five

00:04:52.607 --> 00:04:55.649
itself, same nonce, higher fee, which either

00:04:55.649 --> 00:04:57.706
confirms it or cancels it.

00:04:57.706 --> 00:05:00.781
Once five clears, six and seven follow immediately.

00:05:02.081 --> 00:05:05.176
When you replace a stuck transaction, you actually

00:05:05.176 --> 00:05:08.146
have two choices, both using the same nonce.

00:05:08.146 --> 00:05:11.404
Speed it up: keep the same instruction, just raise

00:05:11.404 --> 00:05:14.706
the fee, so it confirms as originally intended.

00:05:14.706 --> 00:05:17.579
Or cancel it: replace it with a zero-value

00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:20.786
transaction to yourself, at a higher fee, which

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:23.793
uses up that nonce harmlessly and clears the

00:05:23.793 --> 00:05:26.666
jam without ever doing the original action.

00:05:27.966 --> 00:05:29.056
Quick check.

00:05:29.056 --> 00:05:31.741
All your newer transactions are stuck pending.

00:05:31.741 --> 00:05:33.266
What's the most likely cause?

00:05:37.686 --> 00:05:40.291
The answer: an earlier nonce is stuck.

00:05:40.291 --> 00:05:42.824
Replace it or speed it up, same nonce,

00:05:42.824 --> 00:05:46.526
higher fee, and everything behind it follows immediately.

00:05:47.826 --> 00:05:48.776
The real math.

00:05:50.076 --> 00:05:52.101
Here's a real worked example.

00:05:52.101 --> 00:05:55.061
A swap uses 150,000 gas.

00:05:55.061 --> 00:05:59.571
At 20 gwei, a quiet network, that's

00:05:59.571 --> 00:06:04.207
150,000 × 20 = 3,000,000 gwei, which

00:06:04.207 --> 00:06:09.393
is 0.003 ETH. At $3,000/ETH, that's $9.

00:06:09.393 --> 00:06:12.110
The same swap, during congestion,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.168
at 80 gwei, costs $36.

00:06:14.168 --> 00:06:15.333
Same swap.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:16.533
Same wallet.

00:06:16.533 --> 00:06:19.458
Four times the price, because the network got busy.

00:06:20.758 --> 00:06:23.630
And here's the number that changes everything:

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.707
the same swap, on a popular layer

00:06:25.707 --> 00:06:28.268
two instead of Ethereum's main chain.

00:06:28.268 --> 00:06:31.444
Layer 2s batch many transactions together and settle

00:06:31.444 --> 00:06:33.961
them back to Ethereum as one group, which

00:06:33.961 --> 00:06:37.018
typically brings the cost down to somewhere around

00:06:37.018 --> 00:06:38.936
a cent or two, not dollars.

00:06:38.936 --> 00:06:42.261
That's an illustrative, rounded range, real costs

00:06:42.261 --> 00:06:44.654
vary by network and moment, but the

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:47.215
direction is consistent and large.

00:06:47.215 --> 00:06:49.461
It's exactly why Module 0 has you

00:06:49.461 --> 00:06:51.972
practising on a low-fee network first.

00:06:53.272 --> 00:06:54.625
And here's why that number

00:06:54.625 --> 00:06:56.277
matters more than it looks.

00:06:56.277 --> 00:07:00.321
If you're compounding $1.50 of rewards, and gas costs

00:07:00.321 --> 00:07:03.617
$9, the gas costs more than the rewards.

00:07:03.617 --> 00:07:05.667
That's not a rare edge case, it's

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:08.137
the default at small position sizes.

00:07:08.137 --> 00:07:11.322
That's exactly why position size matters on-chain, and

00:07:11.322 --> 00:07:13.697
why this program keeps coming back to it.

00:07:14.997 --> 00:07:16.853
One more real habit: checking

00:07:16.853 --> 00:07:18.947
a transaction on an explorer.

00:07:18.947 --> 00:07:21.892
It shows the status, the fee actually paid, the

00:07:21.892 --> 00:07:25.452
sender and receiver, which contract was called, and every

00:07:25.452 --> 00:07:28.697
token transfer and log the transaction produced.

00:07:28.697 --> 00:07:29.622
Get in the habit of

00:07:29.622 --> 00:07:31.632
checking your own transactions there.

00:07:31.632 --> 00:07:34.402
It's the fastest way to confirm a transaction

00:07:34.402 --> 00:07:37.292
did exactly what you expected, and nothing more.

00:07:38.592 --> 00:07:40.227
Last check, the numbers.

00:07:40.227 --> 00:07:46.027
200,000 gas at 10 gwei, with ETH at $2,500.

00:07:46.027 --> 00:07:46.658
What does that cost? [[pause 4]] The answer: 200,000

00:07:46.658 --> 00:07:47.062
× 10 = 2,000,000 gwei = 0.002 ETH.

00:07:51.482 --> 00:08:04.027
At $2,500/ETH, that's $5.

00:08:05.327 --> 00:08:06.367
One more.

00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:08.575
You're about to sign a transaction, and

00:08:08.575 --> 00:08:10.972
the fee estimate looks unusually high.

00:08:10.972 --> 00:08:12.877
What should you do, in a single word?

00:08:17.297 --> 00:08:18.892
The answer: wait.

00:08:18.892 --> 00:08:21.679
Check the fee estimate before signing, every

00:08:21.679 --> 00:08:23.786
time, and if it's high, a quieter

00:08:23.786 --> 00:08:25.582
moment is often minutes away.

00:08:26.882 --> 00:08:28.775
Put it all together, and here's the

00:08:28.775 --> 00:08:31.442
habit for every transaction you'll ever sign.

00:08:31.442 --> 00:08:33.342
Check the fee estimate first.

00:08:33.342 --> 00:08:34.487
If it looks high and

00:08:34.487 --> 00:08:36.477
you're not racing anyone, wait.

00:08:36.477 --> 00:08:38.048
Sign only once the number makes

00:08:38.048 --> 00:08:39.722
sense for what you're doing.

00:08:39.722 --> 00:08:42.785
And afterward, confirm it on an explorer: status,

00:08:42.785 --> 00:08:45.167
fee paid, and exactly what it did.

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:47.009
Four steps, every time, and

00:08:47.009 --> 00:08:49.312
gas surprises stop being surprises.

00:08:50.612 --> 00:08:52.072
Here's your checklist.

00:08:52.072 --> 00:08:53.607
Do it now, for real.

00:08:53.607 --> 00:08:56.512
First, you check the fee estimate before signing.

00:08:56.512 --> 00:08:58.095
Second, you know how to find

00:08:58.095 --> 00:09:00.142
your transaction on an explorer.

00:09:00.142 --> 00:09:02.451
And third, you know a stuck transaction

00:09:02.451 --> 00:09:04.414
can be sped up or cancelled, with

00:09:04.414 --> 00:09:06.262
the same nonce and a higher fee.

00:09:07.562 --> 00:09:08.807
Let's recap.

00:09:08.807 --> 00:09:11.931
Every transaction runs the same path: create,

00:09:11.931 --> 00:09:15.627
sign, broadcast, mempool, block, confirmed, final.

00:09:15.627 --> 00:09:18.200
Gas is computation, priced as base fee

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.378
plus tip, in gwei, and it's paid

00:09:20.378 --> 00:09:23.317
whether or not the transaction succeeds.

00:09:23.317 --> 00:09:26.585
The nonce orders your transactions strictly, so one

00:09:26.585 --> 00:09:29.587
stuck transaction jams every one behind it.

00:09:29.587 --> 00:09:31.730
And the real cost of a swap can

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.280
swing four times over, quiet versus congested, which

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:37.758
is exactly why position size matters.

00:09:39.058 --> 00:09:41.573
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:09:41.573 --> 00:09:44.555
You now have the whole lesson in your head:

00:09:44.555 --> 00:09:48.349
the seven-stop lifecycle, what gas actually is, why the

00:09:48.349 --> 00:09:52.144
nonce orders everything strictly, how the real cost can

00:09:52.144 --> 00:09:55.261
swing 4x over with congestion and drop by two

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:58.107
orders of magnitude on a layer 2, and how

00:09:58.107 --> 00:10:01.118
to estimate a real cost before you sign.

00:10:01.118 --> 00:10:05.648
Next up, Lesson 1.3: Wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.
